Pityriasis Rosea
Common Infections and Afflictions
Herald patch, Christmas tree distribution, Collarette of scale

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-036
Reason for Visit: Rapid eruption of a widespread, scaly rash causing severe anxiety.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a young adult patient presenting with a sudden, rapid eruption of a widespread, scaly maculopapular rash across the torso over the past 48 hours. The sudden, extensive nature of the rash has caused extreme psychological distress and anxiety regarding a severe, potentially contagious systemic illness (e.g., secondary syphilis or an aggressive drug eruption). Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform a full dermatological examination, definitively exclude highly contagious or life-threatening systemic rashes, and provide immediate clinical reassurance and symptomatic relief. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of escalating patient panic and unnecessary emergency department utilization, strictly fulfilling the criteria for MBS urgent after-hours item numbers 585-600.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: This rash started with one big spot a week ago, and now my entire torso is covered in these scaly red marks. I'm terrified it's spreading to my organs.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 7 days ago with a solitary, large (4cm), erythematous scaly plaque on the right lateral thorax, which the patient initially ignored. Over the last 48 hours, there has been a sudden, widespread subsequent eruption of smaller oval scaly macules and papules strictly confined to the trunk and proximal extremities. The patient reports mild to moderate pruritus, exacerbated by hot showers. Crucially, the patient notes a preceding mild viral prodrome (generalized fatigue, mild sore throat) roughly two weeks ago. The rash exhibits a distinct sparing of the face, palms, and soles. The patient denies any new medications, fever, joint pain, or genital ulcerations, confirming an absolute absence of systemic toxicity.
Functional Status: Independent, but highly anxious and distracted by the visible rash.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute situational distress regarding the sudden disfigurement.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 118/76 mmHg | HR: 84 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, well-perfused. Visibly anxious regarding the dermatological presentation.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. A negative KOH preparation ruling out tinea corporis would assist in confirming the diagnosis if the initial Herald patch was isolated, but the widespread eruption is clinically diagnostic.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination of the torso reveals a classic presentation: a singular, prominent 4cm erythematous "Herald patch" on the right flank. This is accompanied by a diffuse eruption of smaller (1-2cm) oval, salmon-colored macules and papules across the chest, back, and abdomen. The lesions are distributed symmetrically along the skin cleavage lines (Langer's lines), creating a pathognomonic "Christmas tree" pattern on the posterior thorax. Close inspection of the individual oval lesions reveals a distinct collarette of fine, cigarette-paper-like scale just inside the erythematous border. The face, palms, and soles are entirely spared. No oral mucosal lesions. Mild, non-tender cervical lymphadenopathy is present.
Environmental Observations: Safe residential environment.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has aggressively applied harsh antibacterial soaps, incorrectly assuming a staphylococcal infection, which has severely dried and irritated the skin.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Pityriasis Rosea - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Secondary Syphilis - Definitively ruled out by the classic pathognomonic presentation (Herald patch, Christmas tree distribution) and the strict, explicit absence of lesions on the palms of the hands and soles of the feet, which are a hallmark of secondary syphilis.
Guttate Psoriasis - Unlikely given the lack of thick, silvery micaceous scales, the presence of the singular Herald patch, and the distinct collarette scaling pattern (inward facing) rather than whole-lesion scaling.
Tinea Corporis (Widespread Ringworm) - Ruled out clinically as the eruption was sudden and diffuse following a Herald patch, rather than slowly expanding annular rings with central clearing, and the lesions follow cleavage lines.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The explosive appearance of a generalized truncal rash generated severe patient panic, raising valid concerns for aggressive systemic illnesses. Urgent on-site clinical assessment was vital to perform a meticulous physical examination. Recognizing the classic diagnostic triad (Herald patch, Christmas tree distribution, collarette scaling) allowed for immediate, definitive diagnosis of Pityriasis Rosea, a completely benign and self-limiting post-viral condition. By definitively ruling out secondary syphilis or a severe drug reaction, and providing potent clinical reassurance, an unnecessary emergency department presentation was successfully averted. This crucial diagnostic differentiation and anxiety management firmly satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS 585-600 assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Reassurance is the primary therapeutic intervention. Advised Cetirizine 10mg daily to manage the mild to moderate pruritus. Recommended replacing harsh soaps with a gentle, soap-free wash and applying a bland emollient (e.g., Sorbolene) twice daily to counteract the skin dryness exacerbating the itch.
Positive Behavioral Change: To reduce the intense itching, limit your showers to exactly 5 minutes using strictly lukewarm water. Hot water acts as a massive physical trigger, dilating the blood vessels in the skin and releasing a flood of histamine that dramatically worsens the rash and the urge to scratch for hours afterward.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine review with regular General Practitioner in 4 weeks to monitor the expected gradual resolution, or sooner if severe itching prevents sleep.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the rash suddenly spreads to your face, palms, or soles, if you develop painful sores inside your mouth, or if you develop a high fever and joint pain.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical and highly characteristic; however, if the rash persists beyond 12 weeks, a biopsy may be indicated to rule out rare parapsoriasis variants.
Clinical Course: This is a benign, self-limiting condition. The rash will look worse before it looks better, often taking 6 to 8 weeks for spontaneous resolution. It does not leave permanent scars, though temporary hyperpigmentation may occur.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek medical review if the rash becomes intensely, unbearably itchy and fails to respond to oral antihistamines.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: This rash is a delayed, harmless immune system reaction to a common virus you had weeks ago. It is not an active infection, it is not an allergy, and it is absolutely not contagious to others. Your body simply needs time to turn off the alarm.
Actionable Steps: Do not aggressively scrub the scaly spots; friction will prolong the rash. Wear loose, breathable cotton clothing, as synthetic fabrics trap sweat and heat, which act as physical irritants to the inflamed skin.

SMS INFORMATION
